<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest Augustus, King of Hanover</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1771–1851</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ernest Augustus I of Hanover, Duke of Cumberland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest Augustus, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, was the fifth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>. When Victoria became British queen in 1837, Hanover's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> barred her, ending the personal union, and he became king. He revoked the liberal constitution of 1833 and dismissed the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> professors who protested, but later accepted a more moderate constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover.jpg" alt="Ernest Augustus, King of Hanover" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Dawe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernest_Augustus_I_of_Hanover.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
